    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "As we saw in the Drosophila experiments in Figure 3.12, populations generally fluctuate in allele frequency by an amount greater than $ pq/(2N) $. No real population can be expected to satisfy the assumptions of a theoretically ideal population in all respects. Hence, in any actual case, there must be corrections for such complications as fluctuations in population size, unequal numbers of males and females, skewed distributions in family size, population structure, and so forth <sup>[140,181]</sup>. The effects of these complicating circumstances on the change in allele frequencies and rates of allele fixation can be approximated by calculating the effective size of the population and using this value in the theory for an ideal population. That is, the effective population size of an actual population is the number of individuals in a theoretically ideal population having the same magnitude of random genetic drift as the actual population. There are three kinds of effective population size based on how we choose to measure “magnitude,” namely: (1) the change in probability of identity by descent (F), (2) the change in variance in allele frequency, or (3) the rate of loss of heterozygosity. These are called the inbreeding effective size, the variance effective size, and the eigenvalue effective size, respectively.",
